        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.533,
            0.096,
            0.745,
            0.133
          ],
          "content": "How does a switch turn on or off the torchlight?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.747,
            0.066,
            0.88,
            0.189
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.428,
            0.163,
            0.718,
            0.183
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 3.8: Let us construct"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.421,
            0.197,
            0.885,
            0.253
          ],
          "content": " Collect two drawing pins, a safety pin (or a paper clip), two wires, and a small piece of cardboard."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.42,
            0.257,
            0.884,
            0.333
          ],
          "content": " Insert a drawing pin through the ring of the safety pin and fix it to the cardboard piece, ensuring that the safety pin can rotate freely (Fig. 3.11a)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.421,
            0.337,
            0.883,
            0.395
          ],
          "content": " Fix the second drawing pin to the cardboard piece so the free end of the safety pin can touch it (Fig. 3.11b)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.421,
            0.398,
            0.883,
            0.436
          ],
          "content": " Connect a wire to each drawing pin — our switch is ready!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.41,
            0.441,
            0.675,
            0.46
          ],
          "content": "Let us now test our switch."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.427,
            0.47,
            0.663,
            0.492
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 3.9: Let us test"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.421,
            0.506,
            0.883,
            0.546
          ],
          "content": " Connect the electric cell, lamp, and switch as shown in Fig. 3.12a. Does the lamp glow?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.42,
            0.548,
            0.883,
            0.606
          ],
          "content": " Rotate the free end of the safety pin till it touches the other drawing pin as shown in Fig. 3.12b. Does the lamp glow now?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.408,
            0.61,
            0.884,
            0.796
          ],
          "content": "When the safety pin touches both drawing pins, it closes the gap and completes the path, and allows the current to flow. We call this the **ON** position (Fig. 3.12b) where the **circuit is closed** and current flows from the cell’s positive to negative terminal making the lamp glow. When the safety pin does not touch the second drawing pin, the gap in the circuit prevents current flow, and the lamp does not glow. In this **OFF** position (Fig. 3.12a), we say that the **circuit is open**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.796,
            0.884,
            0.892
          ],
          "content": "Note that a switch can be placed anywhere in a circuit. A **switch** is a simple device that either completes or breaks a circuit. The switches used for lights and other devices at home work the same way, though they are designed differently."
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
